<section class="g-paper" id="letter">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow"><span class="tick">\</span> From Akshata</div>
    <h2 style="--d:60ms">I left this industry, and came back.</h2>
    <div class="ab-lg">
      <article class="ab-letter">
        <p class="ab-open" style="--d:100ms">
          I came back because I couldn’t stop believing there was a better way to do this.
        </p>
        <p>
          Twenty years in, the scoreboard had never changed. Number of recruiters. Number of clients. Number of CVs
          sent. Every target I was given measured effort, and every target I handed down did the same. None of them
          measured whether we were right. I didn’t question it for a long time, because nobody did.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can see what that produced. Every year brings more tools, more automation and more profiles reaching more
          people, and a technical hire now takes half as many interviews again as it did in 2021. That is what happens
          when activity is the only thing anyone counts.
        </p>
        <div class="ab-fold open" id="abFold">
          <div class="ab-fold-in">
            <p>
              You know the moment I mean. A profile goes across that reads well, the client brings the person in, and
              somebody asks a real question about the work: what they decided, what happened when it went wrong. Then
              the pause. I’ve been the person in that pause, on both sides of the table. The recruiter was usually good.
              We had sent them in with a CV, an instinct and hope, and called it a process.
            </p>
            <p class="ab-pull">Everybody in that room deserved an answer. Nobody had been given one.</p>
            <p>
              The tools were never made. Nothing helps a recruiter read a job description in a field they have never
              worked in. Nothing turns what a hiring manager means by “strong” into something you can check a person
              against. Other industries built instruments. We hired more people and sent more CVs.
            </p>
            <p>
              I didn’t start as a recruiter. I learnt it, and somewhere in twenty years of doing it hands-on it stopped
              feeling like work. I read a CV, I talk to the person about the technology and the domain, and I know. I
              can count the times I’ve been wrong about someone I put forward, and I don’t need many fingers.
            </p>
            <p>
              That talent was close to useless, because it lived in one head. I could sit with the recruiter next to me
              and talk them through a profile, and by the end of the call they would see what I saw. The next week, on
              their own, they couldn’t do it again. It scaled to as many searches as I could personally sit in.
            </p>
            <p>
              For years I said all of this to anyone who would sit still for it. Almost everyone agreed. Almost nobody
              could act on it, because the pressure to keep delivering numbers never lets up long enough to fix the
              thing producing them. I had lived inside that. Eventually I stopped waiting, and left.
            </p>
            <p>
              Then Abhishek said he could build the part that transfers, and he did. The hundred-plus signals read
              before anyone picks up a phone, the questions written off a candidate’s own CV, the scoring against a bar
              agreed in advance. That now runs at a scale I never could.
            </p>
            <p>
              The rest doesn’t transfer, and we stopped pretending it would. Reading a person is a people problem. So
              the engine hands its work to a senior expert who has done that job, and they do the part no model gets to
              do. There are more than fifty of them now, where there used to be one head.
            </p>
            <p>
              Everything we’ve made is built for the recruiter. That half hour with a candidate is the most valuable
              thing in this business, and the instrument exists to protect it, so the conversation can be about the
              person instead of the logistics.
            </p>
            <p>
              I still run searches myself, with my name on the outcome. The day I stop doing the job is the day I stop
              being useful to the people building the thing that does it.
            </p>
            <p class="ab-close">This industry gave me a career before I had earned one. This is what I owe it back.</p>
            <div class="ab-sign"><b>Akshata Chandavarkar</b><span>Founder &amp; CEO, Tribera</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
